<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Classroom News – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Classroom News</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> We Need Pictures for the Classroom Newsletter.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Clarify personal opinions in group discussions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe plans for the newsletter content (e.g., 'Tomorrow, we will go on a school trip.').
            Can share opinions on others' newsletter ideas and ask follow-up questions (e.g., 'This is a great article. Did you like the topic?').
            Can clarify opinions in group discussions about the classroom newsletter (e.g., 'What do you think about adding more photos?').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various pictures of past school events (e.g., a field trip, an art presentation, a sports event). Students identify the events and briefly say what happened. Teacher asks: "What do you see in this photo?" "When did this happen?" (Narrating past events: 'Yesterday, we went on a field trip to the Emberá Community.') "What exciting event is this?" This activates prior knowledge from previous lessons on newsletter topics and past events.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher shows a picture and models asking for an opinion and clarifying: "This picture shows a fun class event. I think it's a great photo for our newsletter. What do you think about this picture, [Student's Name]? Do you think it's colorful?" The teacher then models responding and asking a follow-up: "Yes, I think it's very colorful! Did you like the topic of this event?" Teacher emphasizes the main verb in future tense contractions: "I'll <b>go</b> on a school trip."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we ask 'What do you think?' to know someone's opinion or to know their name?" (Expected answer: To know their opinion.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'I'll go', am I talking about now or the future?" (Expected answer: The future.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Classroom News")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short dialogue script (role-play format) between two students, Ana and Luis, discussing plans for the classroom newsletter. The dialogue will introduce new vocabulary and target grammar structures in a natural conversation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Dialogue Excerpt:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: none; margin-left: 0; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Ana:</b> "Hey Luis, what will you write for the newsletter this week?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Luis:</b> "I will write the article about our art presentation. It was very creative! But what else do we need?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Ana:</b> "We need pictures for the classroom newsletter! Did you take any photos during the event?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Luis:</b> "Yes, I did! I have some exciting photos. What do you think about adding a column for student ideas?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Ana:</b> "That's a great idea! I think it's a meaningful topic for our readers. I'll organize the layout."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Collaborate in writing projects, such as creating a classroom newsletter, by clarifying roles, responsibilities, and deadlines, and using appropriate language for teamwork.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (oral, T-S/S-S):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What are Ana and Luis talking about?" (Expected: The classroom newsletter.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "Luis will write about a school trip." (False - art presentation) "They need pictures for the newsletter." (True)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> "Who will write the article? Who will organize the layout?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Present simple with questions, Future in affirmative and interrogative sentences, Past simple in affirmative and interrogative sentences. Vocabulary: <i>article, newsletter, pictures, event, topic, layout, take, write, organize, share, discuss, exciting, fun, meaningful.</i> Pragmatic: Describe plans for events; Request materials; Narrating past events.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice: Sentence Scramble & Completion (Pair Work):</b> Students receive cards with scrambled sentences using target grammar and vocabulary (e.g., "I / write / the / article / will"). They unscramble and say the sentence aloud. Then, they complete sentence frames orally in pairs: "I will _____ the _____." (e.g., 'I will write the article.') "We need _____ for the _____." (e.g., 'We need pictures for the newsletter.').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice: Chain Drill & Q&A (Group/Pair Work):</b> The teacher starts a chain: "I will write the article about our school trip." The next student says "What will you do?" and describes their plan using the future tense. Then, pairs ask each other "What else do we need for the newsletter?" and respond using vocabulary (e.g., 'We need a camera to take photos.'). Focus on pronunciation: emphasizing the main verb in 'I'll <b>take</b>', 'We'll <b>share</b>'.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Guided Pair Interaction: Opinion Exchange:</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of picture cards representing different potential newsletter topics (e.g., a picture of students drawing, a picture of a school sports day, a picture of a science experiment). They must discuss which pictures are suitable for the newsletter using opinion phrases and questions. For example, "This is a great photo. Did you like the topic?" "Yes, I did. What do you think about adding this one? I think it's very exciting." They clarify opinions: "Do you mean this picture for the art column?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Classroom News")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Group Discussion and Planning. Students will work in small groups (3-4 students) to plan a section of their upcoming classroom newsletter.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group is assigned a "newsletter section" (e.g., 'School Events', 'Student Art', 'Interviews').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups discuss ideas for articles, pictures, and announcements for their section. They must use the target language to:
                            <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Describe plans for the newsletter content: "I will write the article about our field trip tomorrow."</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Share opinions on others' ideas and ask follow-up questions: "This is a great article. Did you like the topic?" "I think this photo is exciting."</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarify opinions: "What do you think about adding more photos?" "Do you mean a new column?"</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Narrate past events: "Yesterday, we went on a field trip."</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarify roles and responsibilities: "I will be responsible for collecting the pictures." "Can you edit the text?"</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups prepare to briefly present their plan to the class, focusing on key decisions and roles.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher's Role:</b> Circulate, listen, provide support, prompt students to use target language, and encourage interaction and clarification among group members.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on oral participation and a simple peer assessment of group collaboration.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will participate in their group discussions and then present their group's newsletter section plan to the class. The teacher will observe their speaking interactions and use of target language.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Emphasizing the main verb in future tense contractions (e.g., 'I'll <b>go</b>').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> (e.g., <i>article, pictures, event, topic, discuss, add, explain</i>).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity in expressing opinions:</b> (e.g., 'I think this is a great idea.', 'What do you think about...?').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Active collaboration and clarifying opinions:</b> (e.g., asking follow-up questions, clarifying roles and responsibilities).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of grammar structures:</b> Present simple questions, Future affirmative/interrogative, Past simple affirmative/interrogative.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences orally or in writing:
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I learned to talk about..."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I used today was..."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Student and teacher reflection)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of planning the newsletter was the most fun to discuss?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy to ask your classmates 'What do you think?' and clarify their opinions?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson, especially regarding student interaction and clarifying opinions? Students actively used the future tense to describe plans and asked questions using the present simple. Their ability to share and clarify opinions improved.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition)? Some students still need more practice with the pronunciation of short and long vowel sounds in words like 'art' and 'show'. For the next lesson, I will provide sentence frames to guide their writing and ensure they embed the vocabulary and grammar used today into written paragraphs about newsletter content. I will also incorporate exercises focusing on writing opinion statements.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b> This speaking lesson prepared students to orally plan and discuss newsletter content. The next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition) will focus on transforming these spoken ideas and opinions into written articles and descriptive paragraphs for the classroom newsletter, reinforcing the target grammar and vocabulary.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>